  "working_tree": "Uncommitted code-intelligence work; this receipt and documentation follow the frozen checks.",
  "shared_implementation": "Both native workers use the same private creation claim/receipt primitives beneath the existing shared resource interpreter. One version-aware claim gate declines removal without the historical metadata receipt; journal transports do not implement separate recovery policy.",
  "contract": [
    "Actual original-service/HMAC review, SQLite admission and durable active intent precede the real native exclusive creation. An internal durability hook interrupts immediately after creation and before the metadata receipt. Both accepted native adapters previously deleted that same empty inode after an external attribute update and original service/grant expiry.",
    "New private claims carry version 2 inside the existing bounded envelope. Archived claim bodies without the discriminator default to version 1; unknown versions are rejected. This does not change the public intelligence protocol, SDK or database schema.",
    "When a new claim has an unpublished object but no durable metadata receipt, recovery preserves it for inspection and retains the pending journal. A fresh read never becomes the missing historical witness. This also prevents an existing anchor from synthesizing a legacy receipt for a new claim after metadata loss.",
    "Unchanged unwitnessed objects remain pending too. Native accepted contracts cover files and directories, with and without an external attribute update, after provider invalidation and service/grant expiry. Explicit operator inspection/removal is modeled outside automatic recovery; a second real native request then restores the transaction without new action queries, commands or editor publication.",
    "Complete metadata receipts retain normal automatic recovery and the earlier immutable-property checks. A witnessed unpublished file recovers even when creating an anchor is unavailable. Internal-boundary controls explicitly distinguish the pre-receipt inspection case from fully witnessed automatic recovery.",
    "Archived claims retain empty-artifact recovery, including the old anchor-before-receipt order. Future versions and lost new receipts preserve the object and never manufacture a current metadata baseline. Full, partial and Unicode claim/namespace records remain bounded and validated.",
    "Fresh installed Local and Remote resource, command, interrupted recovery and declared-input contracts pass against rebuilt artifacts. The native claim contracts pass on macOS and Linux alongside the shared ownership and original/created metadata contracts."
  ],
